{-
   `@caller` parameters - Design-Test.md §11.1's F2.

   `Caller.yana` beside this file is the same feature stated against `lib/`, and it stays there:
   it asserts through `Show` and `String.unit`, which this stage could not reach at first, while
   it still read a stand-in library of its own. What is here is the half that needs nothing but
   arithmetic, which is why it was split out - and it has stayed split, since it is still a useful
   check on its own terms.

   The compiler fills the position with the same four values a written record of them would be
   built with - a string literal is a borrowed run over a constant global, so a location costs
   some constant data and no runtime work, which is what the `.mir.expect` beside this file
   records.

   Asserted by arithmetic on the filled values rather than by comparing text, because a `String ==`
   between two things the compiler filled would pass with both sides empty.
-}

data Site {file: String, line: Int, column: Int, function: String}
data Where {line: Int}

-- The location, whole. Moving a call below changes the answer, which is the property being tested.
fn lineOf(@caller at: Site) -> Int = at.line
fn columnOf(@caller at: Site) -> Int = at.column
fn functionIs(name: String, @caller at: Site) -> Bool = at.function == name
fn fileWidth(@caller at: Site) -> Size = length(at.file)

-- A subset of the vocabulary. Nothing requires a declaration to ask for all four.
fn shortLine(@caller w: Where) -> Int = w.line

-- The text of what reached another parameter, which is the half a library cannot fake.
fn textWidth(held: Bool, @caller(source: held) text: String) -> Size = length(text)

{-
   A helper that passes its own `Site` on, which is the rule the whole feature rests on.

   A `@caller` parameter may be passed explicitly, by name, and is filled only where it was omitted -
   so this reports the line that called *it* rather than the line inside its own body. That is
   Rust's `#[track_caller]`, and it falls out of "a position no argument reached is filled by its
   default" rather than being a rule of its own.
-}
fn viaHelper(@caller at: Site) -> Int = lineOf(at: at)

-- And the same for the text fill: what reached `held` *here* is the helper's own expression.
fn textViaHelper(n: Int) -> Size = textWidth(n > 0)

fn main() -> Int:
    -- The line of the call and not of the declaration. These two are on consecutive lines, so the
    -- difference is what says the fill is per call rather than per callee.
    let first = lineOf()
    let second = lineOf()
    if second - first != 1 then return 1

    -- Two calls on one line, at different columns. The left one is written first, so its column is
    -- the smaller and the difference is negative - which says the fill is per *call*.
    if columnOf() - columnOf() >= 0 then return 2

    if !functionIs("main") then return 3

    -- The file is the module the call is in. Its spelling is the driver's business, so what is
    -- asserted is that a name got in there at all.
    if fileWidth() == (0 :: Size) then return 4

    if shortLine() != lineOf() then return 5

    -- The text of the expression, which is `1 == 2` - six units, spanning the whole operand and not
    -- just the left one.
    if textWidth(1 == 2) != (6 :: Size) then return 6

    -- Nothing reached the position, so the text is empty rather than a guess.
    if textWidth(True, text: "") != (0 :: Size) then return 7

    -- The helper reports this line, not the one inside its body.
    if viaHelper() != lineOf() then return 8

    -- And the text fill inside a helper is the helper's own expression: `n > 0`.
    if textViaHelper(1) != (5 :: Size) then return 9

    -- A `@caller(source:)` naming a **dot-call's receiver** is `ownership/CallerReceiver.yana` and
    -- not here: the shipping stage used to answer the empty string there, where this stage answers
    -- the text written to the left of the dot - the two never agreed, which is why a shared
    -- fixture must not pretend they did.

    return 0
